21.3 Canonical structure and dataflow

Test IR is a topologically ordered directed acyclic graph. Every step has a unique application-validated stepId, one registered op, and an explicit inputs object. Each input references exactly one literal, immutable binding, or prior step output. A step may reference only an earlier step. Hidden accumulators, implicit stacks, ambient project state, ambient current values, operand-by-position rules, and implementation-dependent input selection are prohibited.

Canonical example:
{
  "version": "closed-loop-test-spec/1",
  "languageVersion": "closed-loop-test-ir-language/1",
  "operationRegistryVersion": "closed-loop-test-ir-operations/1",
  "operationRegistrySha256": "...",
  "steps": [
    {
      "stepId": "S001",
      "op": "LOAD_ARTIFACT",
      "inputs": {
        "binding": {"bindingRef": "PRODUCT"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S002",
      "op": "READ_BYTES",
      "inputs": {
        "artifact": {"stepRef": "S001", "output": "artifact"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S003",
      "op": "DECODE_UTF8",
      "inputs": {
        "bytes": {"stepRef": "S002", "output": "bytes"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S004",
      "op": "PARSE_JSON",
      "inputs": {
        "text": {"stepRef": "S003", "output": "text"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S005",
      "op": "SELECT_JSON_PATH",
      "inputs": {
        "value": {"stepRef": "S004", "output": "value"},
        "path": {"literal": "<registered restricted selector>"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S006",
      "op": "COUNT",
      "inputs": {
        "value": {"stepRef": "S005", "output": "selection"}
      }
    },
    {
      "stepId": "S007",
      "op": "ASSERT_EQ",
      "inputs": {
        "actual": {"stepRef": "S006", "output": "count"},
        "expected": {"literal": 10}
      }
    }
  ],
  "result": {"stepRef": "S007", "output": "assertion"}
}

Every registered operation declares named input ports, output ports, exact types, cardinalities, deterministic error codes, error propagation, and resource-cost category. Each operation declares exact input arity and types and one normalized output type. Multi-artifact branches use explicit step references. BYTE_COMPARE and COMPARE receive explicit left and right inputs. Assertions receive explicit actual and expected inputs. A failed or unknown predecessor prevents dependent execution and propagates the registered terminal disposition without partial canonical result; errors propagate as typed execution failure and no later step may consume a failed output. Intermediate step outputs exist only for the current execution unless explicitly emitted as observations or evidence.

21.14 Resource limits

Centralized hard limits cover:
- total input bytes;
- decompressed bytes;
- IR step count;
- selector depth;
- parsed structure depth;
- collection size;
- regex pattern size;
- regex input size;
- worker execution duration;
- archive expansion where applicable.

The implementation selects explicit constants, centralizes them, documents them, and regression-tests every boundary.
Limits must not be scattered throughout the code.

21.15 Execution dispositions

- Required material unavailable before execution → BLOCKED
- Execution starts but exceeds a runtime limit → EXECUTION_FAILED
- Execution finishes but cannot establish the proposition → UNDETERMINED
- Assertion establishes compliance → SATISFIED
- Assertion establishes noncompliance → VIOLATED

21.16 Test IR language and registry identity

Every Test IR specification and every result binds:
- TEST_IR_LANGUAGE_VERSION;
- OPERATION_REGISTRY_VERSION;
- OPERATION_REGISTRY_SHA256;
- JSON_SELECTOR_REGISTRY_VERSION and digest when used;
- XML_SELECTOR_REGISTRY_VERSION and digest when used;
- REGEX_REGISTRY_VERSION and digest when used;
- parser registry identities used by the execution.

Any operation, argument, dataflow, output, parser, selector, regex, numeric, or error-semantic change creates a new language or registry identity. An affected test or result under an earlier identity becomes stale and cannot satisfy a current proof obligation until explicitly migrated and reverified. Two runtimes MUST NOT claim the same current language identity while accepting materially different operations or semantics.

21.17 Verification timing fields

Every canonical test declares VERIFICATION_PHASE, EARLIEST_EXECUTABLE_STAGE, REQUIRED_BY_STAGE, PER_RUN_REQUIRED, FINAL_PRODUCT_REQUIRED, DELIVERY_REQUIRED, and TARGET_AVAILABILITY_CONDITION. The application validates these values against the test role and proof obligation and derives when execution is due.

14.5 Authoritative field registry

workflow-schema.js MUST expose one closed FIELD_REGISTRY covering every job field, stage field, canonical-record field, nested durable-object field, relationship, prompt field, package field, result field, receipt field, and terminal field. Every field appears exactly once and declares:

- producer;
- value type and exact enum where applicable;
- nullability;
- cardinality;
- requiredness by stage and operation;
- writable operation;
- canonical, proposal, derived-summary, immutable-reference, or audit-only status;
- relationship target and direction where applicable;
- current-scope dimensions;
- migration default or explicit no-default rule;
- invalidation owner;
- normalizer and derivation identities where applicable.

An unregistered field, multiply classified field, ambiguous producer, undeclared default, or operation writing outside its declared set is invalid.

Registry completeness is specification-controlled, not implementation-chosen. Every field named anywhere in this specification receives its complete FIELD_REGISTRY contract either from an explicit statement of this specification or from these closed default rules, applied in order:
1. a field defined in Section 15 uses its Section 15 row and the Section 15 value contracts;
2. STAGE_DECISION, DECISION_EVIDENCE, and every *_DETERMINATION, CONTROLLING_REASON, CONTROLLING_EVIDENCE, and FINAL_* field is an APPLICATION-derived gate summary;
3. every count, coverage, or completeness field — including TOTAL_*, *_COVERAGE, ALL_*, EXPECTED_*, ACTUAL_*, MISSING_*, EXECUTED, SATISFIED, VIOLATED, UNDETERMINED, and RUNS — is an APPLICATION-derived summary under Sections 14.1 and 16.2;
4. every *_RECORDS field is a Section 14.8 projection of its owning Section 38 family, and substantive ownership follows that family's declared partitions;
5. every *_VERSION and *_ID field is an APPLICATION-allocated identity under Section 34.2 or its owning version registry;
6. fields assigned by Sections 14.2, 14.3, and 14.6 follow those assignments;
7. every remaining stage field is an APPLICATION-recomputed summary or immutable reference under Section 14.8; substantive agent or human content never exists as a bare stage field and lives only in its owning family.
A field whose contract is neither explicitly specified nor uniquely produced by these rules is UNDEFINED_FIELD_CONTRACT and blocks specification acceptance; the resolution is a specification change, never an implementation choice.

14.8 Canonical authority and stage-data projections

Canonical record families are the sole substantive authority. A stage field that concerns canonical data is exactly one of:

- an agent proposal before acceptance;
- an immutable ID reference to canonical records;
- an application-recomputed summary derived exclusively from canonical records;
- audit-only historical data that cannot gate.

Stage data MUST NOT duplicate an independently editable substantive canonical value. Any stored projection includes its derivation identity and source-record hashes. A mismatch between a projection and canonical records fails integrity validation and the projection is regenerated or the project is quarantined; the mismatch is never resolved by selecting whichever copy is favorable.

14.9 Durable-object registry

Every durable object required by this specification is either a top-level canonical family listed in Section 38 or a closed nested object registered under exactly one such family. The DURABLE_OBJECT_REGISTRY declares identity, parent family if nested, producer partitions, lifecycle policy, scope, relationships, hash inclusion set, migration behavior, and gate use. No release-bearing search contract, challenge, semantic review, human decision, disclosure authorization, proof obligation, observation, entailment review, expected-variance contract, environment manifest, capability record, backup record, materiality review, derivation record, operation reservation, delivery candidate, delivery record, or delivery-attempt record may exist only as an untyped stageData blob.

16.1 Verification phase and due-stage model

Every release-bearing proposition, proof obligation, test, observation obligation, regression, and deferred failure test declares:

- VERIFICATION_PHASE: PREPRODUCT_ITERATION, FINAL_PRODUCT_DETERMINISTIC, FINAL_PRODUCT_MEANING, FINAL_PRODUCT_ADVERSARIAL, FINAL_REPRESENTATION, DELIVERY_IDENTITY, EVIDENCE_CLOSURE, REGISTRY_CLOSURE, or TERMINAL_DELIVERY;
- EARLIEST_EXECUTABLE_STAGE;
- REQUIRED_BY_STAGE;
- PER_RUN_REQUIRED;
- FINAL_PRODUCT_REQUIRED;
- DELIVERY_REQUIRED;
- TARGET_AVAILABILITY_CONDITION.

The application derives DUE_NOW only when the current stage is at least EARLIEST_EXECUTABLE_STAGE, the target-availability condition is TRUE, the obligation is current and applicable, and the current stage is at or beyond its REQUIRED_BY_STAGE. UNKNOWN target availability blocks only when the obligation could be due.

Stages 12, 13, 18, and 19 evaluate only current applicable PREPRODUCT_ITERATION obligations whose PER_RUN_REQUIRED value is TRUE and that are due by the relevant stage. Final-product, representation, delivery, evidence-closure, registry-closure, and terminal-delivery obligations do not block Stage 18 or Stage 19 before their targets exist, and Stages 12, 13, 18, and 19 MUST NOT require product-only, representation-only, delivery-only, or terminal-only propositions before their targets exist. They remain preserved as future required obligations and become due at their declared stages.

A deferred target-dependent test becomes due no earlier than the first stage where its target exists and no later than REQUIRED_BY_STAGE. It is never required before the target exists and is never represented as executed before actual execution.

17.1 Envelope

Every authoritative external final response is one UTF-8 JSON file whose top-level object conforms to closed-loop-stage-response/3 and contains exactly:

- schema;
- contractProfileId;
- jobId;
- stage;
- operation;
- promptIdentity;
- packageId;
- operationReservationId;
- challengeNonce;
- scope;
- responseType;
- humanInputRequests;
- humanAuthorityCandidates;
- stageData;
- records;
- evidence;
- unresolved;
- warnings;
- attachments.

promptIdentity is one closed object containing instructionId, bodySha256, contractSha256, and contextSignature. Those values MUST NOT be duplicated at the top level. A compatibility import containing duplicated identity values is historical-only unless every duplicate equals the nested value byte-for-byte and an explicit migration rule removes the duplicates before current validation.

Stage-operation contracts determine the exact required, prohibited, and nullable scope fields. Unknown properties are rejected.

18. Human clarification

The human interacts conversationally.
The human MUST NOT be required to construct final-machine JSON during clarification.

For every stage:
- the agent may ask genuinely human-only questions conversationally;
- the agent must not ask for available facts again;
- the agent must not ask for facts reliably obtainable from authorized materials or research;
- later-discovered human-only decisions are asked at the stage where they become necessary;
- one strict final JSON response file is returned only when the stage is ready.

humanInputRequests is the final structured fallback, not the normal conversation format.

Supported fallback controls are:
- TEXT
- LONG_TEXT
- BOOLEAN
- NUMBER
- CHOICE
- MULTI_CHOICE
- DATE
- FILE_REFERENCE

Unknown keys in humanInputRequests are rejected.

Saving a fallback answer uses the SAVE_HUMAN_INPUT command and must:
1. create a new User Job Input version;
2. invalidate the old prompt;
3. invalidate the old proposal;
4. recalculate state;
5. generate a replacement prompt for the same stage;
6. require a new current response.

18.1 External-conversation human-answer capture

When clarification occurs outside the application, the final response may report the answer but cannot write a HUMAN or HUMAN_DECISION field. An external agent may return the answer only as a proposed extracted human-origin value in humanAuthorityCandidates; the application MUST NOT classify that agent-reported value as HUMAN or HUMAN_DECISION until the operator confirms or corrects it in the application. The application compares the reported answer with existing human records. A new human-origin value is presented for confirm or correct and recorded through a dedicated human command. Confirmation MUST be one-tap or direct edit of the proposed value; no retyping is required. Exact confirmation may use atomic co-acceptance under Section 14.10; a correction or inconsistency creates a new input version and replacement prompt. Confirmation does not apply to information already supplied.

31. Ten-run stability and reliability metrics

Exactly ten runs remain required.

The shared repeated-iteration evaluator calculates:

runCount = 10

requirementStability[REQ_ID]:
  satisfied
  violated
  undetermined
  agreementRate

testStability[TEST_ID]:
  satisfied
  violated
  undetermined
  agreementRate

totalDistinctDefects
repeatedDefectCount
uniqueDefectCount
newDefectsByRun[RUN_ID]
unexplainedVarianceCount
requirementsWithCompleteAgreement
requirementsWithDisagreement

Historical or invalidated runs are excluded.
Wrong-iteration and wrong-candidate runs are excluded.

These metrics expose:
- unstable requirements;
- unstable tests;
- stochastic failure concentration;
- novel-defect discovery by run;
- unexplained variance.

They do not create new release thresholds unless existing convergence rules already require the corresponding condition.

Project-level operational metrics include:
- accepted external responses;
- rejected responses;
- stale responses;
- execution failures;
- downstream-discovered silent failures;
- defects caught before baseline;
- defects found after baseline;
- defects found during final verification;
- release reversals;
- artifact identity mismatches;
- regression recurrences;
- human correction frequency;
- native test executions;
- external test executions;
- blocked capabilities;
- evidence-insufficiency failures;
- independence violations.

Browser-local data cannot be represented as population-wide telemetry.

31.1 Closed metric universes

Every coverage, completion, agreement, stability, reliability, and zero-count metric publishes:
- metric identity and derivation version;
- the closed universe definition;
- numerator;
- denominator;
- exact included IDs;
- exact excluded IDs and reasons;
- current scope hash;
- evidence references;
- calculated value and disposition.

An empty denominator is SATISFIED only when a current independently reviewed evidence-supported EMPTY_UNIVERSE determination exists for that metric. Otherwise the metric is UNKNOWN or BLOCKED; it is never automatically 100%. A missing trigger record, missing reservation, missing test, or missing proposition cannot make coverage pass by removing itself from the denominator.

Stage 06 — BUILD THE VERIFICATION SUITE BEFORE WRITING THE PRODUCTION INSTRUCTION
Role: Verification architect
Operations: COMPLETE, PROOF_REVIEW, RECONCILE_VERIFICATION_SUITE
Visible explanation: AI is creating tests now. No tests are executed yet.
The prompt dynamically publishes:
- Test IR version;
- registered operations;
- argument contracts;
- available bindings;
- applicable runtime limits;
- prohibited constructs.
For every mandatory requirement, it must first determine whether the proposition can be faithfully represented in Test IR.
It must perform false-positive analysis.
Completion: Every current applicable mandatory proposition has exactly one current proof obligation and an accepted proof expression; every due test has complete timing, semantic-coverage, evidence, independence, environment, and variance contracts; native tests use the current supported Test IR language and registry identities; unavailable mandatory capability remains blocked.
Fields:
TEST_SUITE_VERSION, TEST_RECORDS, COVERAGE_RECORDS, TOTAL_ACTIVE_MANDATORY_REQUIREMENTS, ACTIVE_MANDATORY_REQUIREMENTS_WITH_AT_LEAST_ONE_READY_TEST, MANDATORY_TEST_COVERAGE, BLOCKED_MANDATORY_REQUIREMENTS, STAGE_DECISION, DECISION_EVIDENCE.
